WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.840
Time travel, finger guns, and buzzing&nbsp;
electricity. No, "Better Call Saul" didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:12.245
become a science fiction series in its finale.&nbsp;
But it did give longtime fans plenty to ponder.

00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:16.240
At the start of Season 6, a worn&nbsp;
paperback copy of H.G. Wells' "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.600
Time Machine" is confiscated from&nbsp;
Saul Goodman's abandoned mansion;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.440
it's only fitting that in the series finale, both&nbsp;
that book and the notion of time travel return in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.040
flashbacks to key moments with Mike Ehrmantraut,&nbsp;
Walter White, and Jimmy's brother Chuck.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.040
While on their desert trek with&nbsp;
Lalo's money from Season 5's "Bagman,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.160
Jimmy asks Mike what he would&nbsp;
do if he had a time machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.720
He poses the same question to Walt while&nbsp;
they await getting disappeared by Ed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.120
vacuum cleaner repairman during the events of&nbsp;
the "Breaking Bad" episode "Granite State."

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.640
Mike, unsurprisingly, thinks of his doomed son&nbsp;
Matty and the first time he ever took a bribe.&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.920
Walt, however, scoffs at the&nbsp;
very idea of time travel, saying:

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:04.080
"You are talking about regret. So if you want&nbsp;
to ask about regrets, just ask about regrets."

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.960
Jimmy gives Mike and Walt shallow&nbsp;
answers to his own question.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.160
But the real answer seemingly comes in the&nbsp;
third flashback as Jimmy stocks groceries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.600
for his brother. Jimmy and Chuck's&nbsp;
relationship was always contentious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.360
with Jimmy often straining for Chuck's approval&nbsp;
and love while not quite getting it. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.920
it's Chuck who makes an effort to&nbsp;
reach out, and Jimmy who refuses it.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.120
"There's no shame in going&nbsp;
back and changing your path."

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.360
"When have you ever changed your path?"

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.120
He leaves Chuck alone to read "The&nbsp;
Time Machine" by lantern light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.131
If Jimmy had accepted that small olive branch,&nbsp;
who knows how things might have turned out.

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:51.600
Wayfarer Airlines flight 515 is an important&nbsp;
part of the "Breaking Bad" mythology. The plane's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.040
fate was a symbol of the way Walter White's&nbsp;
actions had terrible, unexpected consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:03.440
His decision to let Jesse's girlfriend Jane die&nbsp;
leads to her father, an air traffic controller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.840
inadvertently causing a midair collision&nbsp;
that rains down debris over Albuquerque.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.720
Stylistically, the event coined many&nbsp;
of the elements that "Breaking Bad"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.720
and "Better Call Saul" would be known&nbsp;
for, with evocative black-and-white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.920
prologues flashing forward to the disaster and&nbsp;
a secret message hidden in the episode titles.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.040
The Wayfarer disaster was also something that&nbsp;
Saul Goodman milked for all it was worth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.120
wearing a memorial ribbon on his lapel&nbsp;
for the remainder of "Breaking Bad."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.000
In the "Better Call Saul" finale, he is extradited&nbsp;
back to New Mexico to stand trial for his many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
crimes, flying with a U.S. Marshal aboard a&nbsp;
Wayfarer flight. Later, he enters the courtroom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.781
resplendent in a shiny Saul Goodman suit, complete&nbsp;
with a Wayfarer ribbon pinned to the lapel.

00:02:48.958 --> 00:02:52.720
The ghost of Chuck hangs&nbsp;
heavy over the series finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.200
Saul's final courtroom performance has&nbsp;
two major callbacks to his late brother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.760
The most explicit comes as Saul torpedoes&nbsp;
his plea deal by admitting that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.960
not Walter White's unwitting victim, but a&nbsp;
willing participant in his criminal empire.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.760
"That night, I saw opportunity.&nbsp;
A shot at big money."

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.360
Saul soon finds himself talking about Chuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.080
and the shot cuts to an angle high&nbsp;
above the courtroom behind an exit sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.640
buzzing with electricity. That image is a reminder&nbsp;
of Season 3's "Chicanery," in which Jimmy proves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.680
that Chuck's professed electromagnetic&nbsp;
sensitivity was a psychological condition.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:38.080
The meat of Saul's speech, in which he turns&nbsp;
away from a self-serving lie and faces the truth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.400
calls to mind Jimmy's testimony to the&nbsp;
Bar Association at the end of Season 4.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.880
There, he tearfully invoked the name of&nbsp;
his brother as nothing more than a con to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.480
get reinstated as a lawyer, making a fool&nbsp;
of everyone listening, including Kim. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.720
final performance reverses that. He praises&nbsp;
Chuck's brilliant legal mind, acknowledges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.720
his culpability in the events that led to&nbsp;
Chuck's suicide, and reclaims his family name.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:11.360
"The name's McGill. I'm James McGill."
It is all sincere, and it is all for Kim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:18.195
Jimmy's plan all along was not to get himself&nbsp;
off the hook, but to redeem himself in her eyes.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.320
Jimmy's seven-year plea deal at a cushy federal&nbsp;
prison with a golf program goes up in smoke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.760
replaced by an 86-year sentence in a&nbsp;
maximum-security federal prison. But things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.720
could be worse. He finds himself working in the&nbsp;
prison cafeteria, putting his Cinnabon skills to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.400
work baking bread. His long tenure as Saul Goodman&nbsp;
has earned him the respect of his fellow inmates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:46.560
and guards alike, who all refer to him by his&nbsp;
famous alias. And thanks to an unexpected visitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:52.960
he gets to be Jimmy McGill once again, if only&nbsp;
for the length of one smoke. Kim shows up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.560
posing as his lawyer thanks to her New Mexico Bar&nbsp;
Association I.D., which never expired. The two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.240
of them share a cigarette in near silence, just&nbsp;
like they did in their very first scene together.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:10.560
As Kim leaves, likely for the last time, Jimmy&nbsp;
watches from the prison yard and gives her the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:17.200
same finger guns that ended Season 4 and&nbsp;
introduced the legal world to Saul Goodman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:24.080
The same finger guns Kim gave at the end of Season&nbsp;
5 as she devised a plan to ruin Howard Hamlin. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:30.186
while both of those gestures signified the&nbsp;
start of something, this one is a goodbye.

